<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=sexagesimal">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Writing and counting</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sexagesimal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">3rd millennium BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Base 60</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sexagesimal, or base 60, is a numeral system with sixty as its base, which originated with the Sumerians in the 3rd millennium BCE and passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> it was written with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> signs, using ten as a sub-base, and at first had no symbol for zero, so a number's value sometimes had to be judged from its context. Its twelve divisors simplify many fractions, and it survives in the 60 minutes of an hour and the 360 degrees of a circle.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sexagesimal.jpg" alt="Sexagesimal" width="320" height="299" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bill Casselman · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ybc7289-bw.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to Otto Neugebauer, the origins of sexagesimal are not as simple, consistent or singular in time as they are often portrayed. Throughout its centuries of use, sexagesimal notation has always contained a strong undercurrent of decimal notation, and it has always been applied inconsistently, even within a single text. The strongest driver of a fully consistent use was its advantage for writing and calculating fractions, which is why it appears most uniformly in mathematical tables.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The number 60 has twelve divisors: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 10, 12, 15, 20, 30 and 60. With so many factors, many fractions simplify. An hour divides evenly into sections of 30, 20, 15, 12, 10, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2 and 1 minutes, and 60 is the smallest number divisible by every number from 1 to 6. Any fraction whose denominator has only 2, 3 and 5 as prime factors can be written exactly, so that one third is 0;20 and one eighth is 0;7,30.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> the system was not a pure base-60 system with sixty distinct digit symbols. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">Cuneiform</a> digits used ten as a sub-base: narrow wedges for units up to nine and wide wedges for up to five tens. Numbers above 59 were written in blocks in place value notation. With no symbol for zero, the signs for 1 and 60 were identical and a value sometimes had to be judged from context. Later Babylonian texts used a placeholder for zero, but only in the middle of a number, not at its right-hand end.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Old Babylonian Period (1900–1650 BCE) approximated the square root of 2 in sexagesimal. Ptolemy's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almagest">Almagest</a>, written in the 2nd century CE, expressed fractions in base 60, and its table of chords was the only extensive trigonometric table for more than a millennium. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-biruni">Al-Biruni</a> first divided the hour into minutes, seconds, thirds and fourths in 1000, and the Parisian version of the Alfonsine tables of about 1320 recorded fractions of a day in base 60. European astronomers still used it for calculation as late as 1671; Jost Bürgi, in a work presented to Emperor Rudolf II in 1592, used sexagesimal multiplication tables to calculate sines.</p><p>The system survives in a modified form in the 60 minutes of an hour, the 60 seconds of a minute, the 360 degrees of a circle and in geographic coordinates. The modern signs for degrees, minutes and seconds descend from the notation John Wallis generalized in the 17th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sexagesimal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>3rd millennium BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Sumer</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Also arose independently</span>Western New Guinea (the Ekari)</p><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Otto Neugebauer held that the origins of sexagesimal are not as simple, consistent, or singular in time as they are often portrayed, its use always mixing in a strong undercurrent of decimal notation.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">Cuneiform<span>c. 3300 BCE</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/abacus">Abacus<span>2700–2300 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy">Babylonian astronomy<span>c. 1800 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=sexagesimal">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
